---
import "../styles/global.css";

const title = "Finlay McCall — Work better with AI";
const description =
  "Dr Finlay McCall helps people learn to work better with AI, using education to build judgement, routines, and lasting capability.";
const thesisUrl =
  "https://researchprofiles.canberra.edu.au/en/studentTheses/anticipating-curriculum-and-course-design-for-humanai-collaborati/";
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en-AU">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <title>{title}</title>
    <meta name="description" content={description} />
    <link rel="canonical" href="https://finlaymccall.com/" />

    <!-- Open Graph (add an og:image before any wider sharing) -->
    <meta property="og:type" content="website" />
    <meta property="og:title" content={title} />
    <meta property="og:description" content={description} />
    <meta property="og:url" content="https://finlaymccall.com/" />

    <link rel="icon" href="/favicon.svg" type="image/svg+xml" />

    <!-- Fonts. Self-hosting via @fontsource is the perf/privacy upgrade for later. -->
    <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com" />
    <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin />
    <link
      href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Spectral:wght@400;500&family=Inter:wght@400;500;600&display=swap"
      rel="stylesheet"
    />
  </head>

  <body>
    <header class="site-header">
      <div class="container">
        <a class="wordmark" href="/">Finlay McCall</a>
        <nav class="nav" aria-label="Primary">
          <a href="#what-i-do">What I do</a>
          <a href="#approach">Approach</a>
          <a href="#about">About</a>
          <a href="#research">Research</a>
          <a href="#contact">Contact</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </header>

    <main>
      <!-- Hero -->
      <section class="hero" aria-label="Introduction">
        <div class="container">
          <p class="eyebrow">
            Applied human and artificial intelligence, approached through education
          </p>
          <h1>Learn to work better with AI.</h1>
          <p class="lede">
            AI is now part of ordinary work, but most people haven't been taught how to
            use it well. I help individuals and teams understand these systems,
            communicate with them, and build the judgement and routines they need to
            keep learning after I leave.
          </p>
          <a class="cta" data-email href="#contact">
            Email Finlay to start the conversation <span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span>
          </a>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- What I do -->
      <section id="what-i-do" aria-labelledby="what-i-do-title">
        <div class="container">
          <h2 id="what-i-do-title" class="section-title">What I do</h2>
          <p>
            Most people don't need a one-off course on the latest AI tool. They need the
            capacity to keep learning as the tools change.
          </p>

          <p>
            That's where I come in. I work with individuals and teams who want to use
            AI more intelligently in their own context: not by handing judgement over
            to a system, and not by becoming dependent on an outside consultant, but by
            building the habits, confidence, and internal capability to work with AI
            well.
          </p>

          <div class="offer">
            <div class="item">
              <h3>A focused conversation</h3>
              <p>
                For people who are curious, uncertain, or trying to work out what AI
                might mean for their field.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="item">
              <h3>Hands-on coaching</h3>
              <p>
                For individuals or teams who want to improve how they use AI in
                everyday work: writing, research, planning, analysis, communication, or
                internal workflows.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="item">
              <h3>A strategy review</h3>
              <p>
                For organisations that need a clearer view of where AI belongs, where
                it doesn't, and what capability they need to build before adoption
                becomes policy.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p class="closing">
            Whatever the format, the aim is the same. You should come away with better
            judgement, better routines, and more capacity to manage your own
            relationship with AI.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- My approach -->
      <section id="approach" aria-labelledby="approach-title">
        <div class="container prose">
          <h2 id="approach-title" class="section-title">My approach</h2>
          <p>I come to AI through education.</p>
          <p>
            That matters because good AI use isn't just a matter of knowing which tool
            to open or which prompt to copy. It's a learning problem. People need to
            understand what they're trying to do, what the system can and can't help
            with, how to judge the output, and how to keep improving their practice
            over time.
          </p>
          <p>
            My work usually begins with some form of needs analysis. What are you
            trying to do? What do people already know? Where are the risks, gaps,
            habits, and opportunities? From there, I help shape the content, routines,
            evaluation methods, and pedagogy that make AI useful in your setting.
          </p>
          <p>
            That's different from a training course that teaches one tool at one
            moment in time. Tools change. The point is to build capability that
            survives the next product update.
          </p>
          <p>
            This framing grew out of <a href={thesisUrl}>my doctoral research</a> at
            the University of Canberra, where I studied how people and AI systems can
            be prepared to work together deliberately over time.
          </p>
          <p>
            I sometimes describe AI as a language, but that is only part of it. My
            broader focus is applied human and artificial intelligence: the practical
            relationship between people, AI systems, work, communication, learning, and
            judgement.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- About -->
      <section id="about" aria-labelledby="about-title">
        <div class="container">
          <h2 id="about-title" class="section-title">About</h2>
          <div class="about-grid">
            <img
              class="portrait"
              src="/finlay-mccall.jpg"
              width="450"
              height="450"
              alt="Dr Finlay McCall"
              loading="lazy"
            />
            <div class="prose">
              <p>
                I'm Dr Finlay McCall, an independent researcher and educator based in
                Australia. I hold a PhD in Education from the University of Canberra. My
                thesis, <em>Anticipating Curriculum and Course Design for Human-AI
                Collaboration</em>, examined how we design learning for the cognitive
                partnership between people and machines. I continue that line of work
                today through research and practice in human-machine education.
              </p>
              <p>
                Alongside my own practice, I am Project Manager and Research Assistant on
                a University of Canberra and ACT Education Directorate research project on
                teachers' use of generative AI in schools, led by A/Prof Eleni Petraki.
              </p>
              <p>
                Before this work, I spent more than twenty years in education across
                Europe, Asia, and Australia. My background includes work as a CELTA
                Teacher Trainer and IELTS Examiner. I hold a Master of Education
                (TESOL) from the University of Sydney and the Cambridge DELTA.
              </p>
              <p>
                Pedagogy is the thread through all of it. It's the lens I bring to AI.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Selected research and publications -->
      <section id="research" aria-labelledby="research-title">
        <div class="container">
          <h2 id="research-title" class="section-title">
            Selected research and publications
          </h2>
          <p class="intro">
            My work on human-AI collaboration grows out of education research, machine
            education, and human-machine teaming.
          </p>
          <ul class="pubs">
            <li>
              <span class="year">2025</span>
              <span class="cite">
                <strong>PhD thesis.</strong>
                <a href={thesisUrl}
                  ><em>Anticipating Curriculum and Course Design for Human-AI
                    Collaboration</em></a
                >, University of Canberra.
              </span>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="year">2021</span>
              <span class="cite">
                Abbass, H., Petraki, E., Hussein, A., McCall, F., &amp; Elsawah, S.
                (2021). A model of symbiomemesis: machine education and communication as
                pillars for human-autonomy symbiosis.
                <em>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society A</em>.
                <a href="https://doi.org/10.1098/rsta.2020.0364">doi.org/10.1098/rsta.2020.0364</a>
              </span>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="year">2021</span>
              <span class="cite">
                McCall, F., Hussein, A., Petraki, E., Elsawah, S., &amp; Abbass, H.
                (2021). Towards a systematic educational framework for human-machine
                teaming. <em>IEEE International Conference on Engineering, Technology
                &amp; Education (TALE)</em>, 375&ndash;382.
                <a href="https://doi.org/10.1109/TALE52509.2021.9678853">doi.org/10.1109/TALE52509.2021.9678853</a>
              </span>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="year">2020</span>
              <span class="cite">
                McCall, F. (2020). Action research: using Turnitin peer feedback
                assignments to improve academic English writing skills.
                <em>IATEFL Research SIG</em>, 35.
              </span>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="year">2018</span>
              <span class="cite">
                McCall, F. (2018). Using social media in the classroom.
                <em>English Australia Journal</em>, 33(1).
              </span>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="year">2017</span>
              <span class="cite">
                McCall, F. (2017). Using microblogs on Facebook to develop students'
                academic reading and writing skills.
                <em>University of Sydney Papers in TESOL</em>, 12.
              </span>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Contact -->
      <section id="contact" aria-labelledby="contact-title">
        <div class="container">
          <h2 id="contact-title" class="section-title">Contact</h2>
          <p>
            If you're trying to work out where AI fits into your work, the best place
            to start is a conversation.
          </p>
          <p>
            Tell me what you're working on, what you're unsure about, and what you
            want AI to help with.
          </p>
          <div class="contact-lines">
            <a data-email data-email-text href="#"
              ><noscript>hi [at] finlaymccall.com</noscript></a
            >
            <a data-phone href="#"><noscript>Phone available with JavaScript enabled</noscript></a>
            <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/finlay-mccall-39079725/">LinkedIn</a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>
    </main>

    <footer class="site-footer">
      <div class="container">
        <span>&copy; 2026 Finlay McCall</span>
        <span class="links">
          <a href="https://orcid.org/0000-0003-1397-1212">ORCID</a>
          <a href="https://scholar.google.com/citations?hl=en&user=I4O0mMwAAAAJ">Google Scholar</a>
          <a href="https://www.researchgate.net/profile/Finlay-Mccall">ResearchGate</a>
          <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/finlay-mccall-39079725/">LinkedIn</a>
          <a href="https://github.com/drfinlaymccall">GitHub</a>
        </span>
      </div>
    </footer>

    <!--
      Contact obfuscation: the email address and phone link are assembled at runtime
      from parts, so scrapers reading the static HTML do not find a plain mailto or
      tel link. Cloudflare's email obfuscation adds a second layer for the address.
    -->
    <script is:inline>
      (function () {
        var user = "hi";
        var domain = "finlaymccall.com";
        var addr = user + "@" + domain;
        var nodes = document.querySelectorAll("[data-email]");
        for (var i = 0; i < nodes.length; i++) {
          nodes[i].setAttribute("href", "mailto:" + addr);
          if (nodes[i].hasAttribute("data-email-text")) {
            nodes[i].textContent = addr;
          }
        }

        var phoneParts = ["+61", "497", "491", "672"];
        var phone = phoneParts.join(" ");
        var phoneHref = "tel:" + phoneParts.join("");
        var phoneNodes = document.querySelectorAll("[data-phone]");
        for (var j = 0; j < phoneNodes.length; j++) {
          phoneNodes[j].setAttribute("href", phoneHref);
          phoneNodes[j].textContent = phone;
        }
      })();
    </script>
  </body>
</html>
